3. Permitted uses

Personal, internal and production use

You may use Software for personal use, non-commercial use and Your own internal business operations, including production. You may self-host it on Your own equipment or on general-purpose infrastructure You rent from a third party.

You may maintain private forks and modifications for permitted uses. This license does not require publication of private modifications or independently developed applications merely because they use Software.

Infrastructure for Your own applications and services

You may use Software as infrastructure for Your own applications, products and services, including commercial offerings. This includes coordinating people and agents, using HTTP, MCP, CLI or Go interfaces, and delivering application functionality or customer outcomes through an Outcome-as-a-Service product.

You may distribute Software as a component of such an application, including an application installed on a customer's infrastructure, subject to sections 4 and 6. Customers must receive the applicable WOS terms for the included WOS material.

These permissions cover the delivery of Your application's functionality or outcomes. They do not authorize commercial supply of the WOS platform itself described in section 4. An application is not excluded merely because it depends heavily on Software, uses its APIs, exposes an embedded work or progress view, or provides configuration confined to that application's purpose.

Professional services

You may charge for genuine consulting, implementation, integration, training, maintenance or support work on a customer's own permitted deployment. The customer must control that deployment and obtain software rights under the applicable WOS terms. The charge must be for technical work, rather than for a software license, a supplied WOS instance or continued access to the WOS platform.

Renting general-purpose compute, storage or network infrastructure does not itself constitute supplying the WOS platform.

Free distribution

You may publish and share copies, source forks and modifications without charge for non-commercial purposes, subject to the notice and brand requirements below. A free distribution does not authorize recipients to commercially supply the platform.

4. Prohibited commercial supply of the WOS platform

You may not sell, rent, lease, sublicense, commercially redistribute, resell, OEM-supply, commercially white-label, or charge third parties for access to Software itself or to a fork, modification or derivative of it as a platform.

This prohibition covers providing WOS, or a material part of its general-purpose shared-state, coordination, work-authority, planning, delivery, review or outcome-management capabilities, as the software or service being commercially supplied. It includes:

- selling copies, downloads, packages, images, appliances or installations of the platform;
- supplying a WOS instance through a paid hosted or managed service;
- charging for access to a general-purpose WOS workspace, control plane, coordination APIs or work-management capabilities;
- supplying a renamed, rebranded or redesigned derivative platform;
- supplying the platform within a paid bundle, even if no separate price is assigned to WOS.

The prohibition applies to direct and indirect consideration for that supply, including required subscriptions, access plans or bundled charges. Calling the platform free, or describing an access charge as support or consulting, does not change the substance of the transaction.

Names, logos, colors, domains, interfaces, wrappers, deployment models and the absence of a graphical interface do not change this restriction. You need not supply every WOS feature for a platform offering to fall within it.

The permitted applications, component distribution, professional services and general-purpose infrastructure in section 3 remain permitted. The relevant distinction is whether You supply Your own application's functionality or outcomes, or commercially supply WOS platform capabilities themselves.

This license grants no platform resale, reseller, OEM, commercial white-label or paid hosted-platform rights.

5. WOS name, logo and brand

This software license grants no general trademark, service-mark or branding rights. The limited permissions and restrictions in TRADEMARKS.md apply independently.

You may make truthful descriptive references such as "compatible with WOS", "built with WOS" or "forked from WOS" under that policy. You may not use the WOS brand to sell or market the WOS platform as Your own offering or imply that You are WOS or its official representative.

Using another name or logo does not make prohibited commercial supply permissible. A descriptive reference to WOS does not authorize an activity prohibited by this license.

